00:52:37,260 --> 00:52:42,260
So there's a sort of interesting question called memory consistent

546
00:52:42,260 --> 00:52:46,320
models. And here I'll illustrate it with a very simple example.

547
00:52:46,320 --> 00:52:51,320
Imagine we have two global variables, A and B, and we have two different threads.

548
00:52:51,320 --> 00:52:58,350
And so the first thread is going to write, meaning assign a value to A,

549
00:52:58,350 --> 00:53:02,350
and it's going to read, meaning print the value of B.

550
00:53:02,350 --> 00:53:05,350
And the other thread is going to do the opposite.

551
00:53:05,350 --> 00:53:09,350
It's going to write, assign a value to B and print the value of A.

552
00:53:09,350 --> 00:53:12,330
And so now the question is, what are the possible output

553
00:53:12,350 --> 00:53:15,500
outputs for this program.

554
00:53:15,500 --> 00:53:19,300
And so there's a model that sort of the accepted standard

555
00:53:19,300 --> 00:53:23,400
called sequential consistency, which means that these events

556
00:53:23,400 --> 00:53:29,500
can occur, well, that these, within a single thread,

557
00:53:29,500 --> 00:53:34,460
things have to occur in the sequential order of that thread.

558
00:53:34,460 --> 00:53:38,960
But across threads, whether right A or right B occurs

559
00:53:38,960 --> 00:53:41,260
first is completely arbitrary.

560
00:53:41,260 --> 00:53:42,840
And similarly whether writing of

561
00:53:42,860 --> 00:53:52,300
B occurs between these two actions or before is alt-to-arbitrary.

562
00:53:52,300 --> 00:54:12,440
So what it means is you can take two different threads and you can interleave their events in any way, but you should be able to pull out of that interleaving the sequential order of either of both of the two threads.

563
00:54:12,440 --> 00:54:18,200
So when you do that, you end up, you can enumerate in an example like this, all the possibilities.

564
00:54:18,200 --> 00:54:23,400
You can say, well, look at, first is either going to be write A or write B.

565
00:54:23,400 --> 00:54:25,430
Let's pick write A.

566
00:54:25,430 --> 00:54:31,930
So now the next event will be either a read of B or a write of B.

567
00:54:31,930 --> 00:54:39,090
And then if I do write A, write B, then I've completed this thread.

568
00:54:39,090 --> 00:54:43,290
And so now the only possibility is to write to B and read A.

569
00:54:43,290 --> 00:54:46,290
And so forth, you work out all the possible things.

