00:56:29,230 --> 00:56:39,360
So let me just throw, show you a scenario of multi-core hardware that would violate sequential consistency.

592
00:56:39,360 --> 00:56:43,360
Assume that each of our threads has its own private cache.

593
00:56:43,360 --> 00:56:46,090
And so if I, uh,

594
00:56:46,090 --> 00:57:00,830
If I execute this statement, what I'll do is I will grab a copy of A from the main memory and bring it into my cache, and I will assign this new value to it.

595
00:57:00,830 --> 00:57:09,500
And similarly, thread 2 will grab a copy of its, of B and update that.

596
00:57:09,500 --> 00:57:16,660
And now if I do my two print statements, if I do my two print statements, if thread 2 picks up the value from the memory,

597
00:57:16,660 --> 00:57:22,660
from the memory, not knowing that thread 1 has a modified copy of that value,

598
00:57:22,660 --> 00:57:24,660
then it would naturally print 1.

599
00:57:24,660 --> 00:57:30,660
And similarly, if thread 1 picked up a copy of B from main memory, it would print 100.

600
00:57:30,660 --> 00:57:35,660
So we'd see exactly this unallowable execution.

601
00:57:35,660 --> 00:57:41,660
And the reason is, because each of these threads have their own private copies of these shared variables,

602
00:57:41,660 --> 00:57:45,660
and they're not properly synchronized.

603
00:57:45,660 --> 00:57:46,660
But you could.

604
00:57:46,660 --> 00:57:52,320
you could see in a hardware scenario, it would be easy to build this hardware and make that mistake.

605
00:57:52,320 --> 00:57:58,620
So how does it work in a multi-core processor? Well, they have a trick, they call it Snoopy

606
00:57:58,620 --> 00:58:06,120
caches. And it's a little bit like the readers, writers, uh, uh, uh, synchronization that you're

607
00:58:06,120 --> 00:58:11,760
working on for your proxies, that you want to make it so that if everyone's just reading

