00:58:29,950 --> 00:58:36,520
either to read it or to write it, for long enough to make the update.

612
00:58:36,520 --> 00:58:47,330
And so they have a protocol where they tag, actually, and these tags are at the level of cash line.

613
00:58:47,350 --> 00:58:53,650
typically. So the tag a cash line in main memory with its state, and the typical

614
00:58:53,650 --> 00:59:02,290
state would be invalid, it's shared, or it's exclusive. So shared means that there can

615
00:59:02,290 --> 00:59:08,570
be copies of it, but they can only be read-only copies, and exclusive, meaning

616
00:59:08,570 --> 00:59:18,160
that it's exclusively available to a single thread. So this is built into then the hardware of a

617
00:59:18,160 --> 00:59:24,160
a multi-core processor. So what will happen then is in order to do a write to A,

618
00:59:24,160 --> 00:59:31,600
thread 1 will acquire an exclusive copy of this element. And that actually tagging

619
00:59:31,600 --> 00:59:40,290
happens down here at the main memory and in the cache both. And similarly, if thread 2 wants

620
00:59:40,290 --> 00:59:50,840
a to write to be, it must get an exclusive copy of that. And then when the read occurs, what

621
00:59:50,840 --> 00:59:57,610
What happens is actually this cache, miss, will send out a signal on a bus, a shared

622
00:59:57,610 --> 01:00:05,160
communication medium saying, I want to read A. And instead of the main memory responding to it,

623
01:00:05,160 --> 01:00:14,440
actually it will, that result will be supplied by the other cache. And it will convert the state

624
01:00:14,440 --> 01:00:21,520
of this element to being a shared element locally, but you'll see that the main memory element to being a shared element,

625
01:00:21,520 --> 01:00:25,340
element isn't updated yet. It goes through the whole right-back protocol

